# Results

NASA's SPHEREx telescope maps the whole sky in 102 infrared wavelength bands between 0.75 and 5 µm, photographing each position many times over a year. Epoch reads the [[meta.n_frames]] pictures of the first data release (taken [[meta.mjd_min|date]] to [[meta.mjd_max|date]]) directly from NASA's public archive and shows any position in time order. The results below use the same pictures.

## Removing false changes {#which-change-is-real}

SPHEREx takes each picture at a different wavelength with one of six detectors, so a plain blink between consecutive pictures shows many changes where the sky has not changed. Comparing each picture only with pictures from the same detector removes a median [[headline.number]]% of these changes (95% interval [[headline.err_low]]–[[headline.err_high]]%) across [[e2.summary.ratio_corr_over_naive_all.n_fields]] randomly chosen patches ([[e2.total_frames]] pictures). This removes false changes but does not make faint real changes easier to detect: planted sources at 20 times the single-picture noise are recovered at similar rates in both views ([[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M2.efficiency.4|pct]] and [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M1.efficiency.4|pct]]).

![Changes per patch in a plain blink and one detector at a time](figs/v2/fig_e2.svg "Changes flagged in each patch by a plain blink (crosses), one detector at a time (circles) and the experimental colour-aware view (triangles), ordered by Galactic latitude. Counts are summed over all pictures of a patch; the vertical axis is logarithmic.")

## WISE 0855−0714 {#e4}

WISE 0855−0714, the coldest known brown dwarf, is too faint to see in most single pictures. Combining the [[e4.passes.1.n_frames]] pictures from the November–December 2025 visits along its predicted path detects it at [[e4.passes.1.significance|f1]]σ, measured against the same pictures combined along 24 empty paths. Its flux density at 4.4–5.0 µm is [[e4.passes.1.a_hat_mJy|f2]] ± [[e4.passes.1.sigma_a_mJy|f2]] mJy, between the Spitzer and WISE values ([[e4.expected_flux_mJy.IRAC_4p5|f2]] and [[e4.expected_flux_mJy.WISE_W2|f2]] mJy; Luhman & Esplin 2016). Over the [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.n_bins_detected]] wavelength steps where it is detected, the SPHEREx spectrum is [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.mean_ratio_spherex_over_jwst|f2]] ± [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.mean_ratio_err|f2]] times the JWST NIRSpec spectrum (Luhman et al. 2024). The two reddest steps, at 4.8–5.0 µm, fall below it.

![SPHEREx spectrum of WISE 0855−0714 next to JWST](figs/v2/e11_wise0855.svg "SPHEREx spectrum in 0.1 µm steps (circles; open circles are below 3σ; bars are 1σ), the JWST NIRSpec spectrum (thin line) averaged over the same steps (squares), and the Spitzer 4.5 µm measurement (diamond).")

## Distance to Epsilon Indi B {#e12}

Epsilon Indi B is a pair of brown dwarfs that SPHEREx sees as one source. Fitting its SPHEREx positions over a year for position, proper motion and parallax, with nothing taken from Gaia, gives a parallax of [[e12.parallax_mas|f0]] ± [[e12.parallax_err_mas.adopted|f0]] mas, a distance of [[e12.distance_ly|f1]] light-years (68% interval [[e12.distance_interval_ly_68.0|f1]]–[[e12.distance_interval_ly_68.1|f1]]). Gaia measures [[e12.gaia_parallax_mas|f1]] mas. Ordinary stars in the same pictures show a small shared offset that mimics a parallax of [[e12.null.summary.mean_mas|f0]] mas; it is not subtracted, and the quoted uncertainty is the scatter of those [[e12.null.summary.n_used]] stars.

![Epsilon Indi B's path and parallax](figs/v2/e12_parallax.svg "a, positions over a year: single pictures (small points), visit averages (circles), and fits with proper motion only (dotted) and with parallax (dash-dot). b, visit averages with the proper motion removed, and the fitted parallax ellipse. Bars are the standard error of each visit mean.")

## Brown dwarfs classified by methane {#e15}

Methane absorption at 3.3 µm distinguishes T and Y dwarfs from L dwarfs. An index formed from the flux in the 3.2–3.4 µm window relative to the flux on either side classifies [[e15.calibration.loo_accuracy|pct]] of [[e15.calibration.n_decisive]] brown dwarfs with published spectral types correctly when each is left out of the calibration in turn.

![Methane index against colour](figs/v2/e15_methane.svg "The 4.4–5.0 µm to 1.0–1.3 µm flux ratio against the methane index, for brown dwarfs of known spectral type and for candidates without a published spectrum. The dashed line is the dividing value; bars are 1σ.")

## Variability at the north ecliptic pole {#e18}

SPHEREx observes the north ecliptic pole on almost every orbit. Within 0.4° of the pole, [[e18a.data.n_reported]] catalogued sources were measured in [[e18bs.n_pictures|int]] pictures. Half of planted [[e18bs.floor_sin_12_17|pct]] brightness variations are recovered for sources of AB magnitude 12–17, at a false-alarm rate of [[e18bs.fa_rate|pct]]. Most catalogued variables in the field vary by less than this; [[e18bs.allvar.n_above]] of [[e18bs.allvar.n]] pass the threshold.

![Recovery of planted brightness variations](figs/v2/e18b_floor.svg "Fraction of planted variations recovered against source brightness, for sinusoidal variations (a) and eclipses (b) of amplitude 2%, 5%, 10% and 20%. Shaded bands are 95% intervals; the dotted line marks half recovered.")

## Hydrogen emission lines {#e20}

SPHEREx covers the Paschen-α (1.875 µm) and Brackett-α (4.05 µm) hydrogen lines. Of [[e20.M_total]] stars that Gaia classifies as Herbig Ae/Be or Be stars, [[e20.N_total]] show an excess in these lines, against [[e20.control_fp]] of [[e20.control_testable]] ordinary stars of similar brightness in the same pictures. At SPHEREx's spectral resolution, emission from the star cannot be separated from emission by surrounding gas.

![Hydrogen-line excess](figs/v2/e20_lines.svg "a, b, flux relative to the local continuum around Paschen-α (a) and Brackett-α (b) for one candidate (stars) and a control star in the same pictures (crosses); bars are 1σ. c, distribution of the line excess for candidates and controls; the dashed line is the detection threshold.")

## Faint asteroids {#stacking-along-an-orbit}

Combining pictures along a known orbit recovers asteroids that no single picture detects: of [[e13.n_no_single_5sigma]] asteroids with no single detection above 5σ, [[e13.n_combined_ge5]] exceed 5σ when combined. In a blind sample of new asteroids, only [[e14.n_eligible_ge5]] of the [[e14.n_eligible]] expected to reach 5σ did so: the noise in the combined pictures is about 2.5 times the formal errors. Their measured brightness matched the prediction (median ratio [[e14.median_amp_ratio|f2]]).

![Signal-to-noise of an asteroid as pictures are combined](figs/v2/fig_reveal.svg "a, signal-to-noise ratio of asteroid 98397 against the number of pictures combined along its orbit, with the √N expectation. b, signal-to-noise ratio of the single pictures and of empty paths beside the orbit.")

## A search for distant bodies {#distant-bodies}

A body hundreds of AU from the Sun moves on the sky mainly through parallax as the Earth orbits. We searched [[e21.area_deg2|f3]] deg² at the north ecliptic pole, observed in [[e21.n_visits]] visits over [[e21.baseline_days|f0]] days, for sources whose positions follow a parallax ellipse, and found no candidate. A body injected at 500 AU is recovered at a fitted distance of [[e21.check500_fit_au|f1]] ± [[e21.check500_fit_err_au|f1]] AU, and at that distance half of injected bodies with a radius of [[e21.smallest_radius_half_recovered_rearth.radius|f0]] Earth radii (geometric albedo 0.3) are recovered. The field is too small, and single pictures too shallow, to exclude a planet-sized body.

![Recovery of injected distant bodies](figs/v2/e21_completeness.svg "Fraction of injected bodies recovered against heliocentric distance, for seven per-visit signal-to-noise ratios ([[e21.injection_per_cell]] injections per point). Bars are 68% Wilson intervals; the dotted line marks half recovered.")

